    {
      "id": "8pd5486",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.3 Cropping Systems"
    },
    {
      "id": "uo5ak8p",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<strong>Growing a single crop repeatedly; simple but depletes nutrients; increases pest pressure: </strong>Monocropping / Mono-culture",
        "<strong>Two or more crops sown simultaneously without defined row arrangement; risk reduction: </strong>Mixed Cropping",
        "<strong>Two or more crops in same field in definite row arrangement simultaneously; improved land utilisation: </strong>Intercropping",
        "<strong>Growing different crops in succession on same land; breaks pest/disease cycles; improves fertility: </strong>Crop Rotation",
        "<strong>Second crop sown before first is harvested; overlapping growth periods; intensive land use: </strong>Relay Cropping",
        "<strong>Growing two or more crops in sequence on same land in a year; no overlap: </strong>Sequential Cropping",
        "<strong>Food/forage crops grown between hedgerow trees; agroforestry system: </strong>Alley Cropping"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5u7efah",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Benefits of Crop Rotation"
    },
    {
      "id": "w6f13yj",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Restores soil fertility — legumes fix atmospheric N2",
        "Breaks pest and disease cycles (no host continuity)",
        "Reduces weed infestation (different weed spectra)",
        "Improves soil structure (deep vs shallow roots alternate)",
        "Spreads labour and income throughout the year"
      ]
    },
